# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for a -Db_sanitize=thread build, loaded by the
# `tsan` test setup in tests/meson.build (review C item 3.7).
#
# FFmpeg as Ubuntu builds it is not instrumented, so TSan sees its calls to
# free(), memset() and memcpy() but not the atomic reference count in
# libavutil that orders them. With no options the suite reported 746 races at
# 47ffed3 (Ubuntu 24.04, libavutil 58, libswresample 4), each a thread freeing
# or reusing a frame's buffer that an encoder thread had read in
# swr_convert():
#
#   728  free in av_buffer_unref            against  memcpy in swr_convert
#    18  memset in av_samples_set_silence   against  memcpy in swr_convert
#        (a libavfilter pool buffer, reused)
#
# Each entry names the function on the writing side, so a report is dropped
# only when that function is in one of its stacks. Three defects planted in
# the frame handoff (push_frame_to_encs() and fifo_frame.c), each ripped three
# times to FLAC and MP3 at once, all still fail with these entries, and none
# failed under the ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1 they replace:
#
#   * the main thread writing a frame's samples after handing it over:
#     its write against swr_convert() reading them;
#   * the encoders given the frame itself, which the main thread then frees:
#     a heap-use-after-free, the encoder reading the freed frame;
#   * the encoders given the samples without a reference, so the main thread's
#     unreference frees them early: that free is in av_buffer_unref and is not
#     reported, but the block was handed to a later frame while an encoder
#     still read it, and that was (13 runs of 13). ASan and memcheck report
#     this one directly, as a read of freed memory.
#
# tests/tsan_plants.py plants all three in every TSan build's suite and fails
# if one rips without a report, so an entry widened here, or an option that
# hides FFmpeg's accesses again, fails there.
#
# The option dropped every access made inside any uninstrumented library,
# swr_convert()'s reads included, which is the encoder side of the handoff
# this build exists to check.
#
# WHAT THE FIRST ENTRY ALSO HIDES: every free of a frame's or packet's data
# goes through av_buffer_unref(), ours included -- av_frame_free(input) at
# src/cyanrip_encode.c:1149, the 21 other calls of av_frame_free(),
# av_packet_free(), av_packet_unref() and av_buffer_unref() in that file and
# src/coverart.c, and the FREE_FN of fifo_frame.c and fifo_packet.c. A free
# of ours that races a read in our own code is therefore not reported here
# either, as the third planted defect shows. ASan reports it, as a read of
# freed memory, and the sanitizer rows run every test: this build checks the
# reads and writes of a handoff, not its frees.
#
# WHY NOT ANNOTATE OUR FREES INSTEAD (backlog 145, decided 2026-11-03). What
# orders a free is the decrement in av_buffer_unref() that reaches zero, and
# the free follows it inside libavutil. An __tsan_acquire() would have to sit
# between the two, where we cannot put it: one at our call, before the
# decrement, can run before another thread's __tsan_release() and then order
# nothing, so the reports would stay, or, placed to silence them, would order
# accesses the program does not and hide a real race. The way to drop these
# entries is a libavutil built with -fsanitize=thread, which sees the atomic
# itself; that is a build of FFmpeg in the thread row, not done.
race:^av_buffer_unref$
race:^av_samples_set_silence$

# A third, rarer: the main thread's av_buffer_pool_uninit() destroying a
# pool's mutex, when it frees the PCM decoder at the flush
# (cyanrip_send_pcm_to_encoders(), inlined at src/cyanrip_main.c:2080),
# against an encoder thread's earlier pthread_mutex_lock() in
# pool_release_buffer() handing a frame's buffer back (libavutil.so.58 at
# 0x2a324). What orders them is the pool's reference count, decremented by
# the uninstrumented `lock subl` at 0x2a33d; the last to reach zero frees the
# pool. Reproduced once in 72 runs of deemph_with_hdcd, six at a time, on
# 2026-11-02. CI's thread row failed that test with one TSan warning on PR
# #76, its report cut from the log, as our notes record it did on PR #69's.
# cyanrip calls no av_buffer_pool_* function itself; every pool here is
# FFmpeg's own.
race:^av_buffer_pool_uninit$
